 ## What is still open
 
-Existence of a stable probability law for every admissible S1 parameter tuple is not proved here; the stable convolution theorem takes actual probability measures and their characteristic-function identities as explicit premises, and since v0.2.3 those premises are shown to hold for Gaussian laws (`α = 2`) but for no `α < 2`. The Frechet/Gumbel formula identities do not by themselves construct measures; since v0.2.3 they do yield the distribution function of the maximum of independent coordinates that have those distribution functions, and since v0.2.4 the generic maximum/minimum laws are complete (T060 is the first discharged backlog family), but no Frechet, Gumbel or reverse-Weibull measure has been constructed (T061). General infinite/undefined tail indices need a separate extended-real design; the current safe interface concerns finite positive-tail log-ratio limits.
+Existence of a stable probability law for every admissible S1 parameter tuple is not proved here; the stable convolution theorem takes actual probability measures and their characteristic-function identities as explicit premises, and since v0.2.3 those premises are shown to hold for Gaussian laws (`α = 2`) but for no `α < 2`. The generic maximum/minimum laws are complete (T060, discharged in v0.2.4), and since v0.2.5 the Gumbel and Fréchet (`ξ > 0`) probability measures are constructed with their distribution functions identified, so the Fréchet/Gumbel max-stability statements hold for coordinates with those laws under ordinary independence and common-law hypotheses. Still open in T061: the reverse-Weibull measure and the location/scale wrappers for all three families. General infinite/undefined tail indices need a separate extended-real design; the current safe interface concerns finite positive-tail log-ratio limits.
 
 The legacy tangent-only expression and real-valued liminf are preserved for audit comparisons and counterexamples, not as recommended probabilistic definitions. The new subexponential predicate means the heavy-tail convolution class, not the different concentration-theory use of the word subexponential.
 
